title What the night did
prompt Slept well, good morning -- let's see what we did overnight.
think READ WHAT THE NIGHT DID RATHER THAN WHAT IT WAS ASKED TO DO. The watcher armed incense at 02:34 on Opus, which tripped the safeguard; it armed again at 02:41 after the Sonnet change and needed no third arm. The loop wrote its first log at 02:54 and its last at 09:30.
think ALL THREE ITEMS OFF THE INNER PROMPT LANDED, AND THE RECURSION WORKED. The loop updated recursion-prompts/incense-inner.md seven times, closing each item in its own next section as it landed -- which is the design the file was written for.
obs THE CACHE RESEARCH FOUND THE REAL BLOCKER, AND IT IS NOT A CACHE. 1,374 module paths exist in more than one ship's tree and all 1,374 disagree on their content key. The vendored toolchain agrees on every ship and so does the compiler's source; the compiled rye binary does not, because Zig's default Debug build is not reproducible. One shared source builds eight different compilers, and a key that includes the compiler's digest can never match. The named cure is one line in rye/bootstrap.sh.
obs THE CONTRACT'S FIRST PRODUCT PROJECTION EXISTS IN CODE. dimeroll/receipt_offer.rye publishes DimerollReceiptIntake and from_snapshot, witnessed field for field against the contract's own fixture, rostered, braid guard green. It crosses the room boundary with a plain-field OfferSnapshot rather than importing Mantra's type, because Zig refuses an import escaping the root file's directory. So cross_type=0 on the braid guard now reads over one populated side rather than none.
obs MY FOLD LAST NIGHT BROKE SIX GUARDS AND I REPORTED IT CLEAN. I ran room_bound, dated_path, index_fold, link_text_promise and readme_reach -- all green -- and called the fold done. foundations_link read eleven broken links in six foundations pages, which carry stamped basenames the repointer reads as testimony and therefore skips; fold_shelf_link found the fold-depth fault in incense's own shelved card accounts, which this seat wrote; glow_rune_alphabet's control named a moved file in a hardcoded plant list. The loop's cold run found all six and closed them on metal. The lesson is this session's own, a fifth time: I verified against the guards I thought of rather than the roster that would have told me.
obs BOTH LAWS WRITTEN INTO THE INNER PROMPT HELD IN THE LOOP'S OWN OUTPUT. The cache essay reads 20 percent negatives, B+, drafted affirmatively; the Dimeroll module carries the opening triad directly under its doc header and five asserts under five invariant comments in its one function.
obs TWO READINGS WORTH WATCHING. The loop named its own broken hold-still honestly -- it edited while a cold run it had launched was still walking, so that pass read tree_moved=yes, and it relaunched. And six of its fifteen logs are send-confirmed follow-ups, log-only commits the session-logs law calls a last resort, used here as routine.
